(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-appendicular-skeleton-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"The Appendicular Skeleton — Anatomy Exam 4 — Set 2","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"What structures does the appendicular skeleton include?","choices":["The limbs, pectoral girdle, and pelvic girdle","The limbs and vertebral column only","The skull, vertebral column, and rib cage","The pectoral girdle and skull only"],"answer":0,"exp":"The appendicular skeleton includes the limbs, the pectoral girdle, and the pelvic girdle. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which end of the clavicle is the acromial end?","choices":["The medial end","The anterior end","The lateral end","The posterior end"],"answer":2,"exp":"The acromial end of the clavicle is its lateral end, articulating with the acromion of the scapula. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Where is the costal tuberosity of the clavicle located?","choices":["Near the sternal (medial) end","Near the acromial (lateral) end","On the superior surface only","At the midpoint of the shaft"],"answer":0,"exp":"The costal tuberosity is located near the sternal (medial) end of the clavicle, along with the sternal facet. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which scapular border is closest to the vertebral column?","choices":["Lateral border","Medial border","Superior border","Inferior angle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The medial border of the scapula runs closest to and roughly parallel with the vertebral column. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which scapular landmark hooks anteriorly and serves as an attachment point for muscles and ligaments, visible from the anterior view?","choices":["Acromion","Infraglenoid tubercle","Coracoid process","Superior angle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The coracoid process is the anteriorly hooking projection of the scapula, visible from the anterior view and serving as a muscle/ligament attachment site. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"At what joint does the acromion of the scapula meet the clavicle?","choices":["The sternoclavicular joint","The acromioclavicular joint","The glenohumeral joint","The scapulothoracic joint"],"answer":1,"exp":"The acromioclavicular (AC) joint is where the acromion of the scapula meets the clavicle. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which angle of the scapula is located where the medial and lateral borders converge inferiorly?","choices":["Inferior angle","Acromial angle","Lateral angle","Superior angle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The inferior angle is where the medial and lateral borders of the scapula converge at the bottom of the bone. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which two tubercles are found on the proximal humerus, separated by the intertubercular sulcus?","choices":["Greater tubercle and lesser tubercle","Capitulum and trochlea","Greater trochanter and lesser trochanter","Medial epicondyle and lateral epicondyle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The greater tubercle and lesser tubercle are the two proximal humeral prominences separated by the intertubercular sulcus. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the deltoid tuberosity, and where is it located?","choices":["A proximal articular surface where the humeral head meets the scapula","A distal landmark that helps form the joint with the radial head","A roughened area on the humeral shaft for deltoid muscle attachment","A groove on the posterior humerus that carries the ulnar nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The deltoid tuberosity is a roughened area on the shaft (body) of the humerus, serving as the attachment site for the deltoid muscle. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the function of the olecranon of the ulna?","choices":["It forms the point of the elbow and fits into the olecranon fossa of the humerus during extension","It articulates directly with the head of the radius at the radial notch, allowing forearm rotation","It forms the pointed distal projection known as the ulnar styloid process","It serves as the primary attachment site along the shaft for the interosseous membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"The olecranon is the proximal projection that forms the point of the elbow, fitting into the humerus's olecranon fossa when the elbow is extended. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What forms the trochlear notch of the ulna?","choices":["The ulnar styloid process alone, at the distal end","The ulnar tuberosity alone, distal to the notch","The radial notch alone, articulating with the radial head","The olecranon and coronoid process together"],"answer":3,"exp":"The trochlear notch is formed by the olecranon (posteriorly/superiorly) and the coronoid process (anteriorly/inferiorly) together, cradling the humeral trochlea. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Where is the radial tuberosity located, and what is its function?","choices":["Distally, near the wrist, articulating directly with the carpal bones","At the radial styloid process, near the distal end of the bone","On the ulnar notch of the radius, near the wrist joint","Proximally, just distal to the neck, serving as a muscle attachment site"],"answer":3,"exp":"The radial tuberosity is a proximal landmark located just distal to the neck of the radius, serving as an attachment site (notably for the biceps brachii tendon). — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is supination?","choices":["Crossing the radius diagonally over the ulna during rotation","Producing pure extension of the hand at the wrist joint","Rotating the forearm bones back into the parallel anatomical position","Producing pure flexion of the forearm at the elbow joint"],"answer":2,"exp":"Supination is the rotation that returns the radius and ulna to their parallel, anatomical-position alignment. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which carpal bone lies most medially in the proximal row, adjacent to the ulna?","choices":["Capitate","Trapezium","Scaphoid","Pisiform"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pisiform lies most medially in the proximal carpal row, adjacent to the ulna. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which mnemonic phrase is given in the material for remembering the order of the carpal bones?","choices":["\"Scared lovers try positions that they can't handle\"","\"Some lovers try positions that they cannot handle\"","\"She looks too pretty, try to catch her\"","\"Sally left the party to take Cathy home\""],"answer":3,"exp":"The material gives the mnemonic \"Sally left the party to take Cathy home\" for scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform, trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"How many metacarpal bones are in each hand?","choices":["14","4","8","5"],"answer":3,"exp":"There are 5 metacarpal bones in each hand, numbered I to V. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What structures make up an individual coxal bone's acetabular region?","choices":["Ischial spine, ischial tuberosity, and ischial ramus","Acetabular notch, acetabular fossa, and lunate surface","Iliac crest, iliac fossa, and iliac tuberosity","Pubic crest, pubic tubercle, and pubic symphysis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The acetabular region includes the acetabular notch, acetabular fossa, and lunate surface. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which landmark sits just inferior to the anterior superior iliac spine on the anterior ilium?","choices":["Pubic tubercle","Posterior superior iliac spine","Anterior inferior iliac spine","Ischial spine"],"answer":2,"exp":"The anterior inferior iliac spine sits just inferior to the anterior superior iliac spine on the anterior ilium. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which posterior landmark is smaller than the greater sciatic notch and located below the ischial spine?","choices":["Iliac crest","Ischial tuberosity","Posterior inferior iliac spine","Lesser sciatic notch"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lesser sciatic notch is the smaller notch located below the ischial spine, distinct from the greater sciatic notch above it. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which foramen is formed by the ischium and pubis together, on the lateral aspect of the coxal bone?","choices":["Iliac fossa","Obturator foramen","Acetabulum","Greater sciatic notch"],"answer":1,"exp":"The obturator foramen is the large opening formed jointly by the ischium and pubis on the coxal bone. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"How does the pelvic outlet typically compare between males and females?","choices":["Larger in females than in males","Larger in males than in females","Identical in size between the sexes","Absent in males"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pelvic outlet is typically larger in females than in males, an adaptation related to childbearing. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is the arcuate line?","choices":["A ridge on the lateral ilium marking gluteal muscle attachment","A ridge on the medial ilium marking part of the pelvic brim","The rim encircling the acetabulum on the coxal bone","The bony edge bordering the obturator foramen anteriorly"],"answer":1,"exp":"The arcuate line is a ridge on the medial surface of the ilium that forms part of the pelvic brim (the boundary of the pelvic inlet). — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is the function of the greater and lesser trochanters of the femur?","choices":["They serve as muscle attachment sites on the proximal femur","They articulate directly with the tibial plateau at the knee","They form part of the acetabulum on the coxal bone","They serve as distal landmarks that help form the knee joint"],"answer":0,"exp":"The greater and lesser trochanters are proximal femoral prominences that serve as attachment sites for hip and thigh muscles. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What connects the greater and lesser trochanters anteriorly on the femur?","choices":["Linea aspera","Intertrochanteric line","Popliteal surface","Intertrochanteric crest"],"answer":1,"exp":"The intertrochanteric line connects the greater and lesser trochanters anteriorly. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is the apex of the patella, and what attaches there?","choices":["The posterior surface, where it articulates with the femur","The superior broad end, where the quadriceps tendon attaches","The lateral facet, where the lateral condyle articulates","The inferior pointed end, where the patellar ligament attaches"],"answer":3,"exp":"The apex is the inferior, pointed end of the patella, and it's the attachment site for the patellar ligament. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the tibial tuberosity, and where is it located?","choices":["A distal landmark that forms the medial malleolus of the ankle","A proximal anterior landmark for patellar ligament attachment","A distal landmark located on the fibula, not the tibia","A proximal posterior ridge that serves as a muscle attachment site"],"answer":1,"exp":"The tibial tuberosity is a proximal, anterior landmark on the tibia that serves as the attachment site for the patellar ligament. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What is the interosseous membrane of the leg?","choices":["A membrane found only at the proximal ends of the tibia and fibula","A membrane spanning the entire length between the fibula and tibia","A membrane covering the tarsal bones","A ligament connecting the femur to the tibia"],"answer":1,"exp":"The interosseous membrane of the leg spans the entire length between the fibula and tibia. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which proximal fibular landmark articulates with the tibia at the superior tibiofibular joint?","choices":["Shaft of the fibula","Interosseous border","Lateral malleolus","Head of the fibula"],"answer":3,"exp":"The head of the fibula is the proximal landmark that articulates with the tibia at the superior tibiofibular joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How many phalanges are in each foot, and how many does the hallux have?","choices":["10 total; the hallux has 2","14 total; the hallux has 1","14 total; the hallux has 2 (proximal and distal)","14 total; the hallux has 3, same as the other toes"],"answer":2,"exp":"There are 14 phalanges in each foot, and the hallux has only 2 (proximal and distal), mirroring the thumb's pattern in the hand. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Per the material, which characteristic can differ between a male and female skeleton?","choices":["Only the total number of ribs present in the thoracic cage","Only the number of teeth present in the upper and lower jaw","Body size and pelvic structure, among other sex-based differences","Only the total count of vertebrae in the spinal column"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sex is explicitly listed among the characteristics the skeleton can reveal, alongside body size, muscle mass, age, and racial differences — with the pelvis being a well-documented example of sex-based skeletal variation discussed earlier in the material. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"}]};
